# =============================================================================
# This file is for LOCAL DEVELOPMENT only. Copy it to `.env`.
#
# Nothing here is needed to run the app: `docker compose up --build` starts its
# own PostgreSQL and works with no `.env` at all. Copy this one when you want
# accounts, `npm run dev`, or the test suites — all three go through the Supabase
# stack (`npx supabase start`).
#
# The same names live in three other places, and each place owns a different
# subset. Nothing here is read by the deployed app.
#
#   Supabase dashboard  — not env vars at all. It is where the VALUES come from,
#                         and where two settings must be changed by hand. See the
#                         "Supabase dashboard" section at the bottom.
#   Vercel              — the deployed app: Settings -> Environment Variables.
#   GitHub secrets      — CI only, and only the two the `migrate` job uses.
#
# Which variable belongs where:
#
#   name                      local   Vercel   GitHub secret
#   ------------------------  ------  -------  -------------
#   DATABASE_URL                 x       x           x
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL          x       -           x
#   SUPABASE_URL                 x       x           -
#   SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET          x       -           -
#   VITE_SUPABASE_URL            x       x           -
#   VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY       x       x           -
#   CORS_ORIGINS                 x       -           -
#   DOCKER_DATABASE_URL          x       -           -
#   WEB_PORT / API_PORT          x       -           -
#   NODE_ENV / VITE_API_BASE_URL x       -           -
#
# CI's `verify`, `integration` and `e2e` jobs need no secrets: they run against a
# throwaway `postgres:16` service container whose URL is hard-coded in the
# workflow. Only `migrate`, which touches the real database, reads secrets.
# =============================================================================

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Local Supabase stack (`npx supabase start`). No pooler locally, so both URLs are
# the same direct connection.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres

# Read only by docker-compose. Leave it commented and `docker compose up` uses the
# PostgreSQL it starts itself, which is what makes that one command enough for a
# working app. Uncomment to point the containers at the Supabase stack instead —
# the same database the host-side tools and `npm run dev` use.
#DOCKER_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@host.docker.internal:54322/postgres

# The hosted shapes, for reference. On Vercel use the 6543 pooler URL: serverless
# opens a connection per invocation, and `prepare: false` in platform/db/client.ts
# is already set for it. The 5432 direct URL is for migrations only.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# --- API ---------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV=development
API_PORT=3000
# Not needed on Vercel: the SPA and the API are served from one origin there.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# --- Web ---------------------------------------------------------------------
WEB_PORT=8080
VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api

# --- Auth --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave every SUPABASE_* and VITE_SUPABASE_* name blank to run anonymous-only:
# the gate disappears and the API falls back to X-Learner-Id (ADR 0006/0008).
#
# Set, as they are below, they also reach docker-compose: the SPA is built with a
# sign-in gate and the API demands real tokens, so `docker compose up` then needs
# `npx supabase start` beside it. For a compose run that depends on nothing else,
# `docker compose --env-file /dev/null up --build` ignores this file entirely.
#
# Backend, read at runtime. Supabase signs user tokens with ES256 once a project
# has asymmetric keys — the local stack included, despite still printing a
# JWT_SECRET. Setting both is fine: the verifier picks the key from each token's
# own alg header.
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=super-secret-jwt-token-with-at-least-32-characters-long

# Frontend. Vite INLINES these at build time — they are not read at runtime, so a
# Vercel deploy that omits them ships a bundle with no sign-in UI and no gate, and
# adding them later does nothing until the project is redeployed WITHOUT the build
# cache. The anon key is public by design and safe in the bundle; service_role is
# never safe anywhere near this file.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZS1kZW1vIiwicm9sZSI6ImFub24iLCJleHAiOjE5ODM4MTI5OTZ9.CRXP1A7WOeoJeXxjNni43kdQwgnWNReilDMblYTn_I0

# --- API tuning --------------------------------------------------------------
# How long the API may serve authored content (scenarios, questions, the answer
# key, the test bank) from memory. Content changes only when `db:seed` runs, and
# deployments restart the API afterwards; this bounds the case where they do not.
# Set to 0 while authoring content against a long-running dev server.
#CONTENT_CACHE_TTL_MS=300000

# --- Supabase dashboard (settings, not variables) ----------------------------
# Two things the hosted project needs that no env var can express:
#
#   1. Authentication -> Sign In / Providers -> Email -> turn OFF "Confirm email".
#      Hosted projects have it ON by default, unlike the local stack. With it on,
#      sign-up returns no session, the learner is told to check an inbox, and the
#      free tier sends only a few mails an hour.
#   2. If you keep confirmation on instead: Authentication -> URL Configuration ->
#      set Site URL to the deployed domain, or the confirmation links point at
#      localhost.
#
# The project ref in DATABASE_URL and the one in SUPABASE_URL must be the SAME
# project. Different refs means the API verifies tokens against a project that did
# not issue them, and every signed-in request fails with INVALID_TOKEN.
